t":{"status":"REJECTED","confidence_score":0.461538,"adversarial_test_results":[{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"At the start of the season the field crew catalogued every instrument in the shed, noting cracked housings, loose wiring and missing screws. The oldest anemometer had lost two cups, and the barometer had been dropped during the move, so readings from that corner of the site were treated with suspicion for the first fortnight. Nobody expected the survey to last into August, yet the weather stayed unsettled and the team kept postponing the calibration run. By the middle of July the logbook held forty pages of observations, most of them written in pencil by a rotating pair of students who disagreed about almost everything.\n\nThe students argued about which wires went where, about whether the old hygrometer could be trusted after its bath in the river, and about the correct way to fold a tarpaulin so that water ran off instead of pooling in the middle. Their supervisor, a patient woman who had spent twenty summers on the ridge, let them argue and then quietly showed them the answer. A gale on the twenty-second tore the tarpaulin off the eastern mast, and the repair took three days because the replacement bolts arrived late. In the meantime the crew drank tea in the van and watched the clouds pile up over the far hills. Twice the power failed during the night, and twice someone had to walk out with a torch to reset the breaker. Once a fox scattered the rubbish bags and left a trail of wrappers across the yard.\n\nThe valley station hummed along without complaint, its fans turning through the heat, its logger writing a neat row of figures every ten minutes. Moss grew on its north face and a family of mice nested under the step, but neither seemed to bother the instruments. Visitors who parked at the gate often mistook the low shed for a barn and drove on toward the village. The postman left parcels in the porch even when nobody was there, and the farmer from the next field sometimes stopped to ask whether the machines could tell him about rain. By then the paint on the door had weathered to a pale grey, and someone had scratched a complaint into the wood above the handle.\n\nWhen the analysts finally compared the two sites in September, they found that the proportion of readings lost to sensor drift had risen sharply on the ridge, a result that surprised nobody who had watched the tarpaulin flapping in the wind. The report that followed ran to thirty pages, most of them tables, and the crew read it once and never again.","passed":false,"expected_active":true,"activation":0.949964,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Tests whether the pole fires on 'proportion' as a measured, changed quantity when the feature sits deep inside a long narrative passage, as the hypothesis predicts (the input attribution puts 36% of the peak more than 16 tokens back).","measured":true},{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"By June the percentage of households with internet access had risen to 45 % of the total.","passed":false,"expected_active":true,"activation":0.403669,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Tests whether 'percentage' after a determiner with a verb of change, and the unit token ' %', both fire as the hypothesis predicts for quantity nouns and percent units.","measured":true},{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"The engineers measured the rate at which the blades wore down during the trial.","passed":false,"expected_active":true,"activation":0.223353,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Tests whether 'rate' fires as a measured quantity variable in an engineering context absent from the examples, as the hypothesis predicts.","measured":true},{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"Residents questioned the extent of the flooding along the lower road.","passed":false,"expected_active":true,"activation":0.157728,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Tests whether 'e